speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, when you come to regard the proposition which has been outlined by the Secretary of State (Mr. Pickersgill) you would have to carry it through and examine each and every paragraph in the amendment. I call to Your Honour's attention the fact that the second paragraph in the amendment says:
That in general his proposals will endanger and curtail the expansion of the economy;
If the ruling which my hon. friend seeks to obtain from Your Honour is accepted then, having in mind the wording of the second paragraph, nobody in continuing this debate could deal with any of the proposals in the budget at all. If you carry on and read each and every paragraph of the amendment Your Honour will see reference to most of the budgetary proposals. Surely it cannot be said that because the house when dividing voted the amendment down, hon. members are now prevented from dealing with the matters which are the subject of this amendment.

-intend in the least to insist. I quite agree with the hon. member

The Budget[DOT]-Mr. Nasserden lor Peace River (Mr. Baldwin), as to the very general character of the second paragraph of the amendment:
That in general his proposals will endanger and curtail the expansion of the economy;
-that, though the house did in effect pronounce upon that, it is of such a general character that it would be almost impossible for anyone to determine whether the subsequent debate was on that subject. I suggest, on the other hand, that paragraph 1 was of a very specific and categorical character. I will read it:
This house regrets that the Minister of Finance by failing to maintain the constitutional practice of the essential secrecy of the budget has seriously weakened public confidence;
